{"name":"Chimes","hex":"#c7ca86","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chimes","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of chime"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of chime"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chimes"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chime (bell instrument)","blocks":[{"text":"A chime or chime of bells is a carillon-like instrument, i.e. a pitched percussion instrument consisting of 22 or fewer bells. A Chime is primarily played with a keyboard, but can also be played with an Ellacombe apparatus. A Chime is often automated, in the past with mechanical drums connected to clocks and in the present with electronic action. Bellfounders often did not attempt to tune chime bells to the same precision as carillon bells. A chime of bells is defined as specifically having fewer than 23 bells to distinguish them from the carillon. American chimes usually have one to one and a half diatonic octaves. According to a recent count, there are over 1,300 existing chimes throughout the world. Almost all are in the Netherlands and the United States, with most of the remainder in Western European countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/McShane_Eight_Bell_Chime_in_its_frame.jpg/330px-McShane_Eight_Bell_Chime_in_its_frame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chime_(bell_instrument)"},{"title":"Cymbalum","blocks":[{"text":"The cymbalum was the name of two historical instruments, medieval European bells hung and struck with a hammer and Greek kymbalon cymbals. The two instruments may possibly be related, based on the same name being used for both and for the similar \"cup-like shape.\" This relationship in not unique to European bells and cymbals; Persian bells (zang) and cymbals (sanj) share a similar word spread."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Cymbala_and_gigue_from_the_Bout_Psalter_Book_of_Hours.jpg/330px-Cymbala_and_gigue_from_the_Bout_Psalter_Book_of_Hours.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cymbalum"},{"title":"Chimes (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Chimes\" is a song by Scottish artist and producer Hudson Mohawke. The song gained attention after its appearance on the MacBook Air TV ad, as well as after Mohawke's success through the likes of Kanye West and with the TNGHT project."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6d/Hudson_Mohawke_-_Chimes_RMX_single_cover.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimes_(song)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Chronik_um_1370_Cgm_5_Folio_18r_tones_adjusted.jpg/500px-Chronik_um_1370_Cgm_5_Folio_18r_tones_adjusted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Chronik_um_1370_Cgm_5_Folio_18r_tones_adjusted.jpg/500px-Chronik_um_1370_Cgm_5_Folio_18r_tones_adjusted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Set of chime bells (cymbala), circa 1370 A.D. Europeans developed chimes from wracks of small hammer-struck bells into large tower-mounted bells played with mechanized assistance.[10]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChronik_um_1370_Cgm_5_Folio_18r_tones_adjusted.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Cymbala_and_handbells%2C_Bodleian_Library_MS._Laud_Misc._752.jpg/120px-Cymbala_and_handbells%2C_Bodleian_Library_MS._Laud_Misc._752.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Cymbala_and_handbells%2C_Bodleian_Library_MS._Laud_Misc._752.jpg/250px-Cymbala_and_handbells%2C_Bodleian_Library_M